WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.068 --> 00:00:01.368
Crew member: Water!

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.730
Narrator: Look behind the
scenes of this massive tsunami

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.730
in 2012's "The Impossible."

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.620
You'll see that the filmmakers shot it

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.700
on a recreated set at one-third the scale.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:18.330
A smaller set meant less water to control.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.210
Even for shots that weren't
filmed with miniatures,

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.800
the challenge was controlling

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.290
the unpredictable elements of a storm

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:29.760
while making scenes look as
wild and random as possible.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:32.040
These tricks in "The
Impossible" were replicated

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:35.740
and built upon 10 years later
by the same director and DP

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:38.340
to engineer the perfectly chaotic storm

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:40.172
in "The Rings of Power."

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.080
Older movies with limited equipment

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:45.090
had to avoid putting actors and crews

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.640
in real water environments.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:50.820
Instead, films like "Ulysses"
and "Swiss Family Robinson"

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.540
shot ocean storms with model
ships in miniature tanks.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.910
And when you're destroying
a miniature model

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:59.790
that took significant
time and labor to build,

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:01.770
you often only get one take.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.510
Today, films like "The Impossible"

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:07.110
still use scaled-down models
to create ocean storms,

00:01:07.110 --> 00:01:09.110
but they've found new creative ways

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:11.043
around those past hurdles.

00:01:11.043 --> 00:01:14.010
To recreate this 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:15.817
hitting this resort in Thailand,

00:01:15.817 --> 00:01:18.090
"The Impossible" built a model of the site

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:19.770
at one-third the scale,

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.600
the smallest set that would
interact with the wave

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.130
in a convincing way.

00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:25.680
Since they'd have to capture
the shot in one take,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.810
the director of photography, Óscar Faura,

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.830
set up a 10-camera apparatus
to catch every angle.

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.200
And to make sure they
got the wide, ugly impact

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.330
that they wanted on the very first try,

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.697
the team ran extensive
wave-generating tests ...

00:01:39.697 --> 00:01:40.962
Crew member: Agua!

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.160
Narrator: Ultimately achieving
the right level of messiness

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:47.430
by rushing 1,000 tons of
water over the model set

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:51.180
with the power of eight
F1 racing cars behind it.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.280
But the key to getting convincing results

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.070
was combining miniatures
with other methods,

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.640
including the most popular
tool for filming ocean storms,

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.870
giant water tanks.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:04.740
Naomi Watts and Tom Holland
filmed most of their scenes

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.413
at Spain's Ciudad de la Luz studios,

00:02:07.413 --> 00:02:11.250
home to a nearly
400-foot-long seaside tank

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:13.980
capable of rolling out
hundreds of thousands

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.240
of gallons of water.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.910
That kind of control over
water is why Hollywood

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:21.300
most often films its ocean
storms in tanks like this one

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:24.360
or Pinewood Studios' tank
in the Dominican Republic,

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.840
the site for aquatic scenes
in "The Lost City" and "Old."

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.125
The water tank will give you

00:02:30.125 --> 00:02:31.896
all of the control that you need.

00:02:31.896 --> 00:02:34.710
The waves will look the
same every single day.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:38.040
The visibility will be
the same every single day.

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.290
Everything out in the ocean,

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.400
it's just a moving, floating set.

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:43.710
Everything in the water
tank can be serviced

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:45.990
from the parking lot and from the trucks.

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.310
Narrator: Even on a windless day,

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:49.350
the ocean never looks still.

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:52.080
So a tank needs waves and wind to break up

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.830
its mirrorlike surface, which
often means wave machines.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:59.970
These machines plunge into
the water and just send swells

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:02.760
into our set, so it
doesn't look like a pool.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:03.990
Narrator: For "The Impossible,"

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.420
the team modified the
design of the giant tank

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.180
so that it had 33 submergible pumps,

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.980
which would be hidden on camera

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:13.710
and help move over 35,000 gallons of water

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.720
through the tank each day.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.820
All of these techniques came to the fore

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.970
when building the ultimate
storm for "The Rings of Power"

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:23.640
with a team of expert storm makers helmed

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.090
by the director-cinematographer
duo from "The Impossible."

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.960
We went back, and we tore
apart the sequences they shot.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.050
We took the film, and we
went through it beat by beat.

00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:37.590
But for the tsunami, the
water came up slowly.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:40.140
It was more of a rise as a giant wave,

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.517
whereas we were in the midst of the sea,

00:03:42.517 --> 00:03:44.070
in an overwhelming sea.

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:47.100
Narrator: So the team drew
on real-world references too.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:49.560
Ron: We go to storm-chaser material

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.720
and look and see what
it actually looks like.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.090
What does the waves look like at sea

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:56.010
in a giant kind of storm like this?

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.140
Narrator: After extensive
previsualization,

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:00.360
the team tested the
plausibility of the shoot

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.310
with a miniature water tank.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:05.790
Then they shot the episode
in two real water tanks,

00:04:05.790 --> 00:04:08.550
the underwater portion
in a smaller dive tank

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.430
and the rest in a
10-foot-deep outdoor tank

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.880
capable of holding 2 ½
million liters of water.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.730
Over the tank, the team
installed a retractable roof,

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.490
which allowed more control
over the lighting conditions,

00:04:20.490 --> 00:04:23.580
making it easier to extend
the set in postproduction.

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.071
But water doesn't just
absorb light; it reflects it.

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:30.810
So "Rings of Power" swapped out
the color of the backgrounds

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:33.090
depending on the time of
day they were depicting.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:35.760
We had the ability to switch
it to black for night scenes,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.310
to white for the fog scenes.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.860
Narrator: And for day scenes,
they used a blue screen

00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:42.990
that was slightly less blue,

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:45.540
a technique used on "The Impossible."

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:48.420
The VFX team could still
extract actors from it,

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.670
as with a normal blue or green screen.

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.040
But when it was reflected
in the water, it didn't look

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:54.960
like somebody was having
a digital blue party.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:58.260
You know, it looked like kind of sky.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:00.150
Narrator: For the actors inside the tank,

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.620
the team built a raft.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:04.860
Ron: We went out into
the tank with the raft

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:07.470
and started figuring out, safety-wise,

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:10.560
how can we keep the raft
tethered to one spot

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.200
so it doesn't get pushed
around in the waves,

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:15.120
but yet still looks real?

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.310
Narrator: The solution
was a hydraulic base,

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:20.280
like what Dean used on 2018's "Adrift."

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.160
To show the yacht in that movie
going through a hurricane,

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:25.140
Dean's team mounted it on a gimbal

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.990
paired with a robotic 200-ton digger.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.030
On "Rings of Power," the underwater gimbal

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.580
attached to the steel base of the raft.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.820
Dean: It then gets soft,
either balsa or timber

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.770
that goes on top, which
has a soft covering,

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.900
so it's not going to
hurt the actors at all

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.420
when they're rolling and
flopping around on it

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:43.770
and getting washed on and off.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:45.780
Narrator: Safety was paramount here

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.293
because of what the
actors had to stand up to.

00:05:48.293 --> 00:05:51.709
Crew member: Three, two, one, go!

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:53.730
Narrator: Creating all those waves

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.950
required a two-pronged approach.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.530
The team took cues from
earlier work in "Adrift,"

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:01.620
which involved two dump-tank
chutes mounted on trucks,

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:04.380
each holding 4,000 liters of water,

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:08.307
along with a big eight-cannon
air mortar for spray effects.

00:06:08.307 --> 00:06:12.848
That was all way too big for
the scenario, for the raft,

00:06:12.848 --> 00:06:14.373
'cause the raft was 2.4 meters,

00:06:14.373 --> 00:06:17.010
and the boat that we
did was 17 meters long.

00:06:17.010 --> 00:06:18.210
Narrator: So on "Rings of Power,"

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:22.650
the 4,000-liter dump tanks
became 750-liter ones,

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.000
and at the bottom of the chutes,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.400
the team cut little fingers
in the kicker plates

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:27.900
to disperse the water,

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:30.780
which helped make the waves
manageable for the smaller raft

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:32.460
and helped to get the look they wanted.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:35.550
Dean: So it really more
envelopes them in a spray

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.380
than one solid mass of water.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:38.670
Narrator: The dump tanks provided

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:43.020
the specifically timed waves,
but for the continuous chop,

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:46.530
the team hauled in five
14-ton construction diggers

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:48.990
with big steel plates mounted on them,

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:52.290
manually operated from
different spots around the tank.

00:06:52.290 --> 00:06:54.540
There's that much resistance
on the big steel plates

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.730
so that the waves that
they generate would come in

00:06:56.730 --> 00:06:58.980
and they'd pick up and
put a big broken lip

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:01.140
on the top of them that
then catches the wind

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:02.220
and whisks across.

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:04.050
The waves end up coming
back and rebounding,

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.660
and so it makes it really
nicely messy and horrible

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:10.050
once the waves have reached
the optimum sort of size,

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:11.820
and then the dump tank comes in on top.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:13.590
Narrator: The result was convincing,

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:15.060
at least in the close-up.

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:17.525
In a wide shot, we knew that
we were going to have to

00:07:17.525 --> 00:07:20.520
extend and create CG water

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:23.160
and really make foreground waves

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:25.710
that made it even bigger and scarier.

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:27.450
Narrator: But water is famously difficult

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:31.290
to render realistically with
CGI, especially ocean water,

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:34.620
which is constantly transitioning
between different shapes.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.630
So for "Rings of Power,"
the studio ILM created

00:07:37.630 --> 00:07:41.700
a digital ocean machine that
programmed the look of CG water

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:43.830
given a range of variables.

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:46.170
So it was getting a system
together that would allow us

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:48.060
to say, you know, how
choppy should this ocean be?

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:50.160
How windy is it? What time of day is it?

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:52.560
Narrator: Artists could
even include little details

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:54.420
like digital streaks of froth,

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.850
which better ground the waves in reality.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:59.640
It also helped that the
waves only became CG

00:07:59.640 --> 00:08:02.550
around 6 feet outside the actors.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:04.950
The water that actually is touching them,

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:09.450
splashing around the raft
itself, is mostly real.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:10.560
Narrator: Practical flourishes

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:12.540
pulled the whole storm together.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.430
Ron: Anything that touches the actor,

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:17.670
anything that makes hair
move or anything like that,

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:19.740
is always best done practically.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:21.810
So all the rain and the wind

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:23.790
and everything that was
hitting them is practical,

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:25.140
and it needs to be.

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:28.140
Narrator: It also needed to
be powerful. For wind ...

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:31.792
Dean: We have V-8 car engines
mounted on purpose-built fans,

00:08:31.792 --> 00:08:33.600
8- to 10-foot-wide blades

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.090
that push a large volume of wind on cue,

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:39.780
and there was three of those
being used at any one time.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.000
Narrator: To make
torrential pours of rain,

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:44.250
the team could have
used massive sprinklers

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:46.050
or rain-bar structures

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:49.260
but instead opted for
a firefighting method,

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.180
pumping water through the arm of a crane

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.190
into spinning cellar nozzles.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.590
This allowed for 360-degree coverage

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:57.600
and more variable movement.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.730
Dean: It's pushed out
over the filming set,

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:01.590
usually a little bit towards the wind,

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:03.360
so that by the time it hits the actors

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.970
it's caught with the wind
and goes over the raft.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:08.400
Narrator: All that rain was
clearly caught on camera

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:09.960
thanks to the controlled lighting

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:11.790
that backlit the water droplets

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:14.220
and also helped the flashes of lightning

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:16.560
translate powerfully on-screen.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:17.910
Those were pyro effects

00:09:17.910 --> 00:09:20.550
set off through a remote firing system,

00:09:20.550 --> 00:09:23.640
which provided a second reason
for the retractable roof.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.048
Ron: When you do lightning
strikes, they illuminate

00:09:26.048 --> 00:09:28.090
so broadly that you want to make sure

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:31.590
the light's contained and
pushed down into the set.

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:34.620
This way, we got maximal
strength from our lightning.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:37.367
Narrator: With so much done
practically on "Rings of Power,"

00:09:37.367 --> 00:09:40.050
the VFX team could focus on the details,

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:42.300
like adding these sharp lightning bolts,

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:44.070
painting out cameras and equipment

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:45.630
reflected in the water's surface,

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:49.680
and then seamlessly weaving
together the real and CG water.

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:52.380
The fact that storms are chaotic by nature

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:55.020
goes a long way to tying
all the effects together,

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:57.750
from waves to wind to rain.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.850
Ron: The great thing about a storm at sea

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.615
is the sea becomes very
choppy and the wind is blowing

00:10:02.615 --> 00:10:05.670
and there's lots of stuff
going on in multi directions.

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:07.170
Made it a little bit easier.

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:09.150
Narrator: Of course, creating these storms

00:10:09.150 --> 00:10:10.980
only looks like chaos.

00:10:10.980 --> 00:10:13.710
So it's all a very
choreographed sort of thing

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:14.670
to bring together.

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:15.840
It's like a song.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:19.248
You always have to hit the
same beats over and over.

